  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\n(Sunday Night - Tuesday)\n\nThere is little/no change in the ambient air mass through Monday \nnight, so we are still expecting very mild nights (lows in mid to \nupper 70s) along with at least one more dangerously hot/humid day on \nMonday. Similar to Sunday though, I am not sure that it will stay \ndry areawide. There may be thunderstorms across the mid Missouri \nValley and/or Upper Midwest that could at least try and spill \ninto the region with north/northwest flow aloft. Some guidance \n(such as the ECMWF) also shows afternoon development along/east \nof the Mississippi River (again, near that same surface trough\naxis). For now, kept this period dry given the low confidence, \nbut we may need to add PoPs on Monday. The chances of storms \nbeing widespread and early enough to really negatively impact \nheat index values is very low though, so no changes to heat \nheadlines for Monday. It is possible though that some counties \nmay need to be upgraded to a warning if confidence in at least 2\ndays of 110+ HI values increases.\n\nForecast confidence in temperatures/heat index values on Tuesday \nremains low due to differences in the timing of the synoptic cold \nfront. The ECMWF remains 12-18 hours faster than the GFS. These \ndifferences should get ironed out over the next 36-48 hours. The \nadvisory though may need to be extended another day for at least \nportions of the area, with better chances the farther south you go.\n\n\n(Tuesday Night - Next Saturday)\n\nConfidence is fairly high that the dangerous heat and humidity will \nend across the entire area by Wednesday, with even the slower GFS \nprogressing the front through the region by late Tuesday night. \nTemperatures (and humidity levels) are expected to drop back closer \nto normal values for late July/early August. The chances for more \nwidespread showers/thunderstorms may also tend to increase as the \nmid/upper level ridge shifts more toward the desert southwest. This \nmay allow for \"ridge-running\" shortwaves to produce rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms that could traverse the region.",
